Switch: read Aurora's per-draw uniform buffer through a 64 KiB window

Every GX draw has its own uniform record, so Aurora sets its uniform bind group at a new dynamic
offset for almost every draw (1549 UBO binds in a 1553-draw Outset frame). Each became a
glBindBufferRange; in Mesa 20.1 the next draw then re-validates both stages' constant buffers
and nvc0 emits a MEM_BARRIER 0x1011 (invalidating the shader instruction, data and constant
caches) before it. With the window:

- PipelineLayoutGL picks the layout's first dynamic-offset uniform buffer binding;
- the pipelines of that layout get one more internal immediate, uniformWindowBase, placed right
  after the user immediates so that both go up in the one glUniform1uiv the draw makes anyway;
- Tint (UniformWindowOptions) decomposes that binding to array<vec4u, 4096> and adds the
  immediate to the index of every load;
- the bind group tracker binds 64 KiB from the record's offset (clamped to the buffer's end) and
  only rebinds when a record leaves the window; the draw passes the record's position in the
  window (in 16-byte units) as the immediate.

Checked with switch/dawn/gltest (Dawn GL on Mesa llvmpipe): 1024 draws over two passes with a
per-draw record at moving offsets, records repeated, the second pass near the buffer's end,
pipeline switches between uniform layouts; pixel-exact against a CPU reference with the window
(then a run-time toggle) off and on (and failing when the window offset is forced to 0); the 823 GX shaders of a Mac run
to Outset translate, compile and link with the window on.
